oindent	extbf{Background.} Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\cite{deepseekmath_2024,deepseekR1_2025} drives recent advances in mathematical reasoning for sub-3B language models, but a recent reproducibility analysis~\cite{sober_look_2025} shows that small-model RL gains often fail to survive multi-seed evaluation. The roles of training-language choice and auxiliary reward components remain understudied at the deployment-relevant single-GPU LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} scale. 
oindent	extbf{Methods.} On the DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B base, we run a controlled multi-seed GRPO study under a single A100 80\,GB LoRA constraint ($r{=}16$). Three primary arms vary exactly one axis at a time: \armA{} (English data, $R_1{+}R_2$), \armB{} (Vietnamese-translated data, identical rewards), and \armC{} (English data plus a fastText-based~\cite{joulin2017fasttext} language-consistency reward~$R_5$). Each arm is trained with three random seeds. A three-seed constant-bias ablation \armD{} tests whether the auxiliary-reward effect reduces to reward magnitude. We evaluate on AMC-23, MATH-500, AIME-2024, and the 10-language MGSM benchmark~\cite{mgsm_2022}. 
oindent	extbf{Results.} We report three orthogonal findings. 	extbf{(i)~Positive.} On AIME-2024 	exttt{maj@8} -- the hardest benchmark in our suite -- \armC{} achieves the highest mean accuracy ($37.8 \pm 1.9$\,\%) and the only positive effect over the untrained base ($+4.4$\,pp), robust across three seeds. 	extbf{(ii)~Methodological.} \armA{} exhibits $\sigma{=}11.3$\,pp seed variance on AMC-23 and consistently degrades AIME-2024 	exttt{pass@1} (mean $-12.2$\,pp, $3/3$ seeds), confirming that single-seed claims at sub-3B + LoRA scale are unreliable. 	extbf{(iii)~Negative.} On 10-language MGSM, all four training conditions converge to within $\pm 0.5$\,pp of the untrained base mean, indicating that training-language choice does not produce cross-lingual transfer at this scale. The constant-bias ablation \armD{} fails to reproduce \armC{}'s effect on AIME-2024 	exttt{maj@8} ($\armC{}{-}\armD{}{=}{+}5.58$\,pp, $95\%$ bootstrap CI $[+1.13, +11.10]$, excluding $0$), refuting a pure reward-magnitude explanation. 
oindent	extbf{Conclusion.} The auxiliary reward $R_5$ improves the hardest-benchmark capability while preserving multilingual performance, but its contribution is content-specific rather than a pure reward-magnitude perturbation. We release all code, configurations, LoRA adapters, evaluation JSONs (with full 	exttt{responses[]} arrays), and per-step reward traces to support independent replication. \end{abstract} % ===================================================================== \section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro} Reinforcement learning from verifier signals has become the dominant paradigm for improving mathematical reasoning in mid-sized language models. GRPO~\cite{deepseekmath_2024,deepseekR1_2025} is the workhorse of this paradigm: by sampling a group of candidate completions per prompt and assigning advantages relative to the group mean, it removes the need for a learned value head while preserving on-policy PPO~\cite{schulman2017ppo}-style updates. Open-RS~\cite{openRS_2025} shows that GRPO can lift a 1.5\,B distilled checkpoint to $80$\,\% on AMC-23 with only 50 optimization steps, $7$\,K training problems, and four NVIDIA A40 GPUs. Two structural assumptions are baked into nearly every published GRPO recipe at this scale: (i)~the training corpus is monolingual English; and (ii)~the reward sum is dominated by accuracy and format components. The behaviour of GRPO when either assumption is perturbed has received limited systematic attention at the sub-3\,B scale most relevant to deployed systems and to independent researchers on commodity cloud budgets. This paper supplies a multi-seed controlled study at that scale, building on a single-seed preliminary preprint~\cite{dang_v1_preprint_2026}.  oxedclaim{% 	extbf{Main claim.} Multi-seed evaluation reveals three orthogonal patterns. (i)~The language-consistency reward arm \armC{} achieves the highest mean AIME-2024 	exttt{maj@8} ($+4.4 \pm 1.9$\,pp over base across three seeds). (ii)~Vanilla English GRPO \armA{} exhibits $\sigma{=}11.3$\,pp seed variance on AMC-23 and consistently degrades AIME-2024 	exttt{pass@1} by $-12.2 \pm 7.7$\,pp. (iii)~On 10-language MGSM, all training conditions converge to within $\pm 0.5$\,pp of the untrained base. A three-seed constant-bias control \armD{} fails to reproduce \armC{}'s effect on AIME-2024 	exttt{maj@8} ($\armC{}{-}\armD{}{=}{+}5.58$\,pp, $95\%$ bootstrap CI $[+1.13, +11.10]$), refuting a pure reward-magnitude explanation.} \paragraph{Why sub-3B and a single GPU?} Sub-3B reasoning models are the deployment frontier for resource-constrained applications. We deliberately target the hardware ceiling at which most independent researchers can reproduce GRPO results: a single A100 80\,GB GPU on commodity cloud infrastructure. Open-RS~\cite{openRS_2025} reports $80$\,\% AMC-23 	exttt{maj@k} with $4{	imes}$A40 full-parameter training ($192$\,GB total VRAM); we use LoRA $r{=}16$ as the natural fallback at one-quarter VRAM and reach $57.5$\,\% 	exttt{pass@1} (or $75.0$\,\% 	exttt{maj@4} on Open-RS's own released RS2 checkpoint, within $5$\,pp of their headline), placing the residual gap on training capacity (LoRA vs full-parameter) rather than the evaluation pipeline. \paragraph{What differs from prior work.} Open-RS~\cite{openRS_2025} does not vary training language or reward function. \citet{xlingual_collapse_2025} report cross-lingual collapse at $3$\,B+ but vary only the test language. M-Thinker~\cite{mthinker_2025} works at sub-3\,B but focuses on Chinese-only multilingual training. Our three-arm comparison (\armA{}~EN, \armB{}~VI, \armC{}~EN${+}R_5$) is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study at this scale where exactly one axis (language or reward) is changed at a time on a single base model and budget. \paragraph{Contributions.}  egin{enumerate}\itemsep0pt \item Multi-seed empirical evidence (3 seeds $	imes$ 4 arms) that single-seed GRPO results at sub-3\,B + LoRA scale are unreliable ($\sigma{=}11.3$\,pp on AMC-23 for vanilla English training). \item Documentation of \armC{}'s positive effect on the hardest benchmark: $+4.4 \pm 1.9$\,pp on AIME-2024 	exttt{maj@8} over the untrained base, robust across three seeds. \item A three-seed constant-bias ablation (\armD{}) that fails to reproduce \armC{}'s AIME-2024 	exttt{maj@8} result ($\armC{}{-}\armD{}{=}{+}5.58$\,pp, $95\%$ bootstrap CI $[+1.13, +11.10]$, excluding $0$), demonstrating that $R_5$'s contribution is content-specific rather than a pure reward-magnitude perturbation. \item A 10-language MGSM evaluation showing that the GRPO recipe at this scale \emph{preserves but does not improve} multilingual capability regardless of training-language choice. \end{enumerate} % ===================================================================== \section{Related Work} \label{sec:related} \subsection{GRPO for small reasoning models} DeepSeekMath~\cite{deepseekmath_2024} introduced GRPO, replacing PPO's learned value head with a group-relative baseline. DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekR1_2025} scaled this technique and produced distilled checkpoints (including the 1.5\,B model used here) that have become standard sub-3\,B baselines. Open-RS~\cite{openRS_2025} explored which hyperparameters lift a distilled 1.5\,B to $80$\,\% on AMC-23 within 50 steps; we adopt their RS2 recipe verbatim for \armA{}. Variance- and length-normalized variants -- Dr.GRPO~\cite{drgrpo_2025} and DAPO~\cite{dapo_2025} -- are orthogonal to the axes we vary. \subsection{Cross-lingual mathematical reasoning} Multilingual mathematical reasoning has been studied primarily at $7$\,B and above. LinguaLIFT~\cite{lingualift_2024} proposes a two-stage instruction tuning framework. MAPO~\cite{mapo_2024} and MathOctopus~\cite{mathoctopus_2024} fine-tune open models for multilingual mathematical reasoning at 7\,B scale. \citet{xreasoning_test_time_2025} examines inference-time multilingual reasoning via test-time scaling. The multilingual GSM benchmark~\cite{mgsm_2022} is the de-facto evaluation harness; OpenMathInstruct-2~\cite{openmathinstruct2_2024} supplies English-only training data. Cross-lingual collapse~\cite{xlingual_collapse_2025} is the closest concurrent work, observing language drift during GRPO at $\geq 3$\,B; our setting differs in three respects: (i)~sub-3\,B with a single-GPU LoRA budget where prior baselines underperform their reported numbers; (ii)~a controlled \emph{training-language} ablation in addition to test-language variation; and (iii)~explicit reward intervention as a treatment. M-Thinker~\cite{mthinker_2025} is the closest scale-matched work but evaluates only on Chinese. \subsection{Auxiliary rewards and reward shaping} Reward shaping is well-studied in classical RL. \citet{ng1999policy} proves that potential-based shaping preserves the optimal policy, while \citet{skalse2022rewardhacking} characterize how mis-specified auxiliary rewards can induce reward hacking. \citet{zheng_intrinsic_2018} study learned intrinsic rewards in policy gradients and provide constant-bias baselines that are conceptually analogous to our \armD{} ablation. In language-model fine-tuning, auxiliary rewards are typically interpreted as injecting content-level signal into the policy gradient. To our knowledge, no prior work examines what happens when an auxiliary reward fires nearly uniformly across training samples and therefore carries minimal content information. The \armC{} arm provides a natural setting to test this regime; the \armD{} ablation (Section~ef{sec:a4_ablation}) operationalizes the prediction. % ===================================================================== \section{Preliminaries} \label{sec:preliminaries} We restate the relevant pieces of the GRPO objective to fix notation for the mechanistic discussion in Section~ef{sec:discussion}. \paragraph{GRPO objective.} Let $\pi_	heta$ denote the current policy and $\pi_{	heta_{	ext{old}}}$ the behaviour policy at the start of the optimization step. For each prompt $\mathbf{p}$, GRPO samples $G$ completions $\{\mathbf{c}^{(1)},\ldots,\mathbf{c}^{(G)}\}$ and computes a scalar reward $R(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{c}^{(g)})\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ per completion. The group-relative advantage is  egin{equation} A^{(g)} =  rac{R(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{c}^{(g)}) -  ar{R}_{\mathbf{p}}}% {\sigma_{R,\mathbf{p}} + \varepsilon}, \label{eq:advantage} \end{equation} where $ ar{R}_{\mathbf{p}}$ and $\sigma_{R,\mathbf{p}}$ are the within-group mean and standard deviation. With per-token importance ratios $ho_t^{(g)}$ clipped via the standard PPO trust region~\cite{schulman2017ppo}, the GRPO loss is  egin{align} \mathcal{L}_{	ext{GRPO}}(	heta) &= \E_{\mathbf{p}}\Big[\sum_g \sum_t \min\! ig(ho_t^{(g)} A^{(g)}, 
onumber\ & \quad \mathrm{clip}(ho_t^{(g)},1{-}\epsilon,1{+}\epsilon) A^{(g)} ig)\Big] 
onumber\ & \quad -  eta_{	ext{KL}}\,\KL(\pi_	heta\,\|\,\pi_{	heta_{	ext{old}}}). \label{eq:grpo} \end{align} \paragraph{Advantage invariance under additive offsets.} With multiple reward components $\R{1},\ldots,\R{K}$ and weights $w_k$, the per-completion reward is $R(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{c}) = \sum_k w_k \R{k}(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{c})$. For any constant $b\in\mathbb{R}$,  egin{equation} A^{(g)}\!\left[R + bight] =  rac{(R + b) - ( ar{R}+b)}{\sigma_R + \varepsilon} = A^{(g)}\!\left[Right], \label{eq:advantage-invariance} \end{equation} i.e., adding a constant offset to every completion's reward leaves the advantage unchanged. This is the textbook reason ``trivially-satisfied'' auxiliary components are believed inert. Equation~\eqref{eq:advantage-invariance} ignores the rest of the optimization machinery -- in particular the clipping ratio $ho_t^{(g)}$ and the KL penalty -- which depend on \emph{realized rewards}, not advantages, and are therefore \emph{not} invariant to additive offsets. Section~ef{sec:discussion} returns to this point. % ===================================================================== \section{Method} \label{sec:method} \subsection{Base model and training pipeline} All arms start from the publicly-released \path{deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B} checkpoint~\cite{deepseekR1_2025}. We do \emph{not} use a separate SFT stage (matching Open-RS): training is GRPO directly from the distilled base. Each cell is a single (arm, seed) pair, producing one LoRA adapter at step~50. \subsection{Reward functions} \label{sec:rewards} All arms share two rewards (Open-RS RS2 setup):  egin{itemize}\itemsep0pt \item $\R{1}$~	extbf{correctness}: indicator of Math-Verify~\cite{mathverify_2024} equivalence between the extracted answer $\hat{y}$ and the gold $y^\star$. Extraction priority: 	exttt{<answer>} tag, then 	exttt{	extbackslash boxed\{\}}, then last-number fallback. \item $\R{2}$~	extbf{format}: indicator that the completion matches a regex requiring both 	exttt{<think>...</think>} and 	exttt{<answer>...</answer>} blocks, in that order; matches the training system prompt template. \end{itemize} Arm \armC{} adds the language-consistency reward $\R{5}$:  egin{equation} \R{5}(\mathbf{p},\mathbf{c}) =  egin{cases} 0 & 	ext{if } |\mathbf{c}| < 10,\[2pt] \mathbf{1}[\mathrm{lang}(\mathbf{c}) = \mathrm{lang}(\mathbf{p})] & 	ext{otherwise,} \end{cases} \label{eq:r5} \end{equation} where $\mathrm{lang}(\cdot)$ is the fastText~\cite{joulin2017fasttext} 	exttt{lid.176.bin} language identifier, and the $|\mathbf{c}|{<}10$ guard accounts for fastText's unreliability on very short strings. With English training data the model rarely code-switches; thus $\R{5}\approx 1$ for almost every training generation, and $\R{5}$ provides effectively zero content-discrimination signal in this regime. We discuss the implication in Section~ef{sec:discussion}. \subsection{Three primary arms}  egin{description}\itemsep0pt \item[\armA{} (English-only)] training data 	exttt{knoveleng/open-rs} (7\,K English problems, no SFT); rewards $\R{1}+\R{2}$. \item[\armB{} (Vietnamese-translated)] training data \path{5CD-AI/Vietnamese-MetaMathQA-40K-gg-translated} (5{,}203 records extracted from a 7\,K random subset by retaining only those whose Vietnamese response contains the answer marker ``\DJ'ap 'an l\`a''); rewards $\R{1}+\R{2}$. \item[\armC{} (English with $\R{5}$)] same training data as \armA{}; rewards $\R{1}+\R{2}+\R{5}$ with $\R{5}$'s 	exttt{no\_penalty\_for\_en} flag disabled so it can fire on every prompt. \end{description} A fourth ablation arm \armD{} replaces $\R{5}$ with a deterministic constant reward of $1.0$; we describe it together with the mechanism hypothesis in Section~ef{sec:a4_ablation}. \subsection{Compute and hyperparameters} Hardware: $1	imes$ NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80\,GB on commodity cloud infrastructure. LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}: $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, dropout~$0.05$, 	exttt{bias=none}, target modules $\{q,k,v,o\}\_\mathrm{proj}$. GRPO: $G{=}6$, 	exttt{max\_completion\_length}$=3584$, 	exttt{temperature}$=0.7$, $ eta_{	ext{KL}}=0.04$, learning rate $10^{-6}$, scheduler 	exttt{cosine\_with\_min\_lr} (	exttt{min\_lr\_rate}$=0.1$, warmup $0.1$). Effective batch is 96 prompts (per-device 6 $	imes$ gradient accumulation 16), 	exttt{bf16}, flash-attention~2, gradient checkpointing. Training uses TRL~\cite{trl_2025} 0.15.2 with in-process vLLM~\cite{kwon2023vllm} 0.7.2 rollout (	exttt{gpu\_memory\_utilization}$=0.25$, leaving $\sim 60$\,GB for training). We save at step 50 (Open-RS RS2 peak). \subsection{Evaluation} We use the Open-RS 	exttt{lighteval} 	exttt{MATH\_QUERY\_TEMPLATE} verbatim (no system prompt; evaluation template embedded in the user message), with greedy decoding ($T{=}0$), 	exttt{max\_tokens}$=8192$, and no early stop. Aligning to this template closes the AIME-2024 gap to Open-RS-reported numbers from $-10$\,pp to $-2.1$\,pp. Benchmarks: AMC-23~\cite{amc23_dataset} (40 problems, 	exttt{pass@1} and 	exttt{maj@4}), MATH-500~\cite{lightman2024verify} (500, 	exttt{pass@1}), and AIME-2024~\cite{aime2024_dataset} (30, 	exttt{pass@1} plus 	exttt{maj@8} with seeds 42--49), and MGSM~\cite{mgsm_2022} (250 problems each in 10 languages, 	exttt{pass@1}). % ===================================================================== \section{Experiments}

