ewcommand{ oxedclaim}[1]{%  egin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!4,colframe=blue!50!black,boxrule=0.5pt]% \small #1 \end{tcolorbox}} 	itle{Beyond English-Only GRPO: Training Language and Auxiliary Reward as Implicit Regularizers in Sub-3B Math Reasoning} \author{Vu Dang \ 	exttt{[email]} \ \ Code: \url{https://github.com/anonymous/xling-grpo-sub3b}}  egin{document} \maketitle % =====================================================================  egin{abstract} We study Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) post-training of a 1.5B distilled reasoning model under a single-A100 LoRA constraint, comparing three arms that vary exactly one axis: training language (English vs Vietnamese-translated) and reward function (with vs without a fastText-based language-consistency reward~$R_5$). On the DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B base, English-only GRPO gains 7.5 percentage points (pp) on AMC23 but loses 16.7 pp on AIME-2024 --- a benchmark-specific overfit at 50 steps. 	extbf{Adding the language-consistency reward $R_5$, even when it fires uniformly at 1.0 on English training data, recovers 13.3 pp on AIME-2024 and produces the highest mean accuracy across four hard math metrics.} Vietnamese-translated training data shows a milder version of the same regularization effect. Both findings hold on a \$20 single-GPU LoRA budget, suggesting that auxiliary reward components and out-of-domain training data offer cheap regularization mechanisms for sub-3B GRPO. We release training/eval scripts, all checkpoints, and per-step reward traces. \end{abstract} % ===================================================================== \section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro} GRPO~\cite{deepseekmath_2024,deepseekR1_2025} drives recent gains in mathematical reasoning for sub-3B models, but training corpora and reward functions are overwhelmingly English. Prior work observes that GRPO at moderate scales causes \emph{cross-lingual collapse}~\cite{xlingual_collapse_2025} and benchmark-specific overfit~\cite{openRS_2025}. We isolate two cheap interventions --- changing the training language or adding a language-consistency reward --- and ask whether either acts as an effective regularizer at sub-3B scale.  oxedclaim{% 	extbf{Main claim.} On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B with a 50-step Open-RS Stage-1 GRPO recipe (LoRA $r{=}16$, single A100), pure English GRPO trades AMC23 gains for AIME-2024 losses (+7.5 pp / $-$16.7 pp). Adding a fastText language-consistency reward $R_5$ --- without changing training data --- recovers 13.3 pp on AIME-2024 and yields the highest mean accuracy across four math benchmarks. Vietnamese-translated training data shows the same regularization signature at smaller magnitude. } \paragraph{Why sub-3B and a single GPU?} Sub-3B reasoning models are the deployment frontier for resource-constrained applications. We deliberately target the budget ceiling at which most independent researchers can reproduce GRPO results: a single A100 80GB on commodity cloud ($\sim$\$1.50/h). Open-RS~\cite{openRS_2025} reports 80\% AMC23 with 4$	imes$A40 full-parameter training (192 GB total VRAM); we use LoRA $r{=}16$ as the natural fallback at one-quarter VRAM and reach 57.5\%, a 22.5 pp gap that we document explicitly. \paragraph{What we find different from prior work.} Open-RS does not vary training language or reward function. \citet{xlingual_collapse_2025} report cross-lingual collapse at 3B+ but vary only test language, not training language. M-Thinker~\cite{mthinker_2025} works at sub-3B but focuses on Chinese-only multilingual training. Our three-arm comparison --- \armA{} (EN), \armB{} (VI), \armC{} (EN+$R_5$) --- is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study where exactly one axis (language or reward) is changed at a time on a single base model and budget. \paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A reproducible single-GPU LoRA GRPO pipeline that achieves $+7.5$ pp on AMC23 over base (vs Open-RS reported $+17$ pp on 4$	imes$A40); we document the gap honestly. (2) Empirical evidence that English-only GRPO at 50 steps causes \emph{benchmark-specific overfit}: gains concentrate on AMC23, losses concentrate on AIME-2024. (3) A surprising finding: a language-consistency reward $R_5$ --- even firing uniformly at 1.0 on English data --- acts as an \emph{implicit regularizer}, recovering 13.3 pp on AIME-2024 and improving mean accuracy. We hypothesize this is a reward-magnitude effect on PPO clipping geometry, not a content effect. % ===================================================================== \section{Related Work} \label{sec:related} \paragraph{GRPO for small reasoning models.} DeepSeekMath~\cite{deepseekmath_2024} introduced GRPO; DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekR1_2025} scaled it. Open-RS~\cite{openRS_2025} demonstrates 80\% AMC23 pass@1 on a 1.5B distilled checkpoint (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B) with 7K problems and 50 GRPO steps. We adopt their hyperparameter recipe (RS2 setup: 	exttt{accuracy}+	exttt{format} rewards, weights $[1.0, 1.0]$, $G{=}6$, max\_completion 3584, learning rate $10^{-6}$) verbatim for our \armA{} arm. Variance and length normalisation variants---Dr.GRPO~\cite{drgrpo_2025} and DAPO~\cite{dapo_2025}---are orthogonal to our axes and not used. \paragraph{Cross-lingual reasoning.} LinguaLIFT~\cite{lingualift_2024} and \citet{xreasoning_test_time_2025} explore two-stage instruction tuning and test-time scaling at 7B+. MGSM~\cite{mgsm_2022} is the standard multilingual math benchmark; OpenMathInstruct-2~\cite{openmathinstruct2_2024} provides English-only training data. Cross-lingual Collapse~\cite{xlingual_collapse_2025} is the closest concurrent work, observing language drift during GRPO at 3B; our setting differs in three ways: (i) sub-3B with a deliberate single-GPU LoRA constraint where prior baselines underperform their reported numbers, (ii) controlled \emph{training-language} ablation rather than only test-language variation, and (iii) explicit reward intervention as a treatment. M-Thinker~\cite{mthinker_2025} is the closest scale-matched work but evaluates only on Chinese; our setup admits direct extension to other languages. % ===================================================================== \section{Method} \label{sec:method} \subsection{Base model and training pipeline} All three arms start from 	exttt{deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B}~\cite{deepseekR1_2025}, the strongest publicly-available sub-2B distilled reasoning checkpoint. We \emph{do not} use a separate SFT stage (matching Open-RS): training is GRPO directly from the distilled base. Each cell is a single (arm, seed) pair: $	extsc{base} \xrightarrow{	ext{50-step GRPO}} 	extsc{ckpt-50}$. \subsection{Reward functions} \label{sec:rewards} All arms share two rewards (Open-RS RS2 setup):  egin{itemize}\itemsep0pt \item $R_1$ 	extbf{correctness}: $\mathbb{1}[	ext{Math-Verify}(\hat{y}, y^\star)]$, extracting $\hat{y}$ from 	exttt{<answer>} tag, then 	exttt{	extbackslash boxed\{$\cdot$\}}, then last-number fallback. \item $R_2$ 	extbf{format}: $\mathbb{1}[	ext{regex match 	exttt{<think>...</think>...<answer>...</answer>}}]$, matching the training system prompt template. \end{itemize} Arm \armC{} adds $R_5$ 	extbf{lang-consistency}: \[ R_5(	extbf{p}, 	extbf{c}) =  egin{cases} 0 & |	extbf{c}| < 10 	ext{ tokens (langID unreliable)} \ \mathbb{1}[	ext{fastText}(	extbf{c}) = 	ext{lang}(	extbf{p})] & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \] fastText langID uses the public 	exttt{lid.176.bin} model. Note: with English training data, $R_5$ fires uniformly at 1.0 across nearly all generations (model rarely code-switches on English prompts). We discuss the implication in §ef{sec:discussion}. \subsection{Three arms}  egin{description}\itemsep0pt \item[\armA{} (English-only):] training data = 	exttt{knoveleng/open-rs} (7K English problems, no SFT). Rewards: $R_1+R_2$. \item[\armB{} (Vietnamese-translated):] training data = 	exttt{5CD-AI/Vietnamese-MetaMathQA-40K-gg-translated} (5{,}203 records extracted from a 7K random subset, after filtering responses that lack the ``Đáp án là'' answer pattern). Rewards: $R_1+R_2$. \item[\armC{} (English + $R_5$):] same training data as \armA{}, rewards $R_1+R_2+R_5$ with $R_5$'s ``no\_penalty\_for\_en'' flag disabled so it can fire on every prompt. \end{description} \subsection{Compute and hyperparameters} 1$	imes$ NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB on Vast.ai. LoRA $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, dropout 0.05, target = $\{q, k, v, o\}$\_proj. GRPO: $G{=}6$, 	exttt{max\_completion\_length}{=}3584, 	exttt{temperature}{=}0.7, $ eta_{	ext{KL}}{=}0.04$, learning rate $10^{-6}$, 	exttt{cosine\_with\_min\_lr} (min\_lr\_rate=0.1, warmup 0.1), effective batch 96 prompts (per\_device\_batch 6 $	imes$ grad\_accum 16), bf16, flash-attention 2, gradient checkpointing. vLLM rollout uses 	exttt{gpu\_memory\_utilization}=0.25 to leave 60 GB for training. We save at step 50 (Open-RS RS2 peak). \subsection{Evaluation} We use the Open-RS 	exttt{lighteval} 	exttt{MATH\_QUERY\_TEMPLATE} verbatim (no system prompt, evaluation template embedded in the user message), with greedy decoding ($T{=}0$), 	exttt{max\_tokens}{=}8192, no early stop. Aligning to this template closes the eval gap to Open-RS reported numbers on AIME-2024 from $-$10 pp to $-$2.1 pp. Three benchmarks: AMC23~\cite{amc23_dataset} (40 problems, pass@1), MATH-500~\cite{lightman2024verify} (500, pass@1), AIME-2024~\cite{aime2024_dataset} (30, pass@1 + maj@8 with seeds 42--49). % ===================================================================== \section{Experiments} \label{sec:experiments} \subsection{Three-arm results} Table~ef{tab:three_arm_results} reports pass@1 on the three benchmarks for the untrained base, our three trained arms, and Open-RS RS2's reported numbers. \input{tables/table_results} \input{tables/table_delta_vs_base}  egin{figure*}[t] \centering \includegraphics[width=0.95	extwidth]{figures/fig1_arm_comparison.pdf} \caption{Three-arm GRPO comparison on three math benchmarks (single seed). Bar height: pass@1 (or maj@8 for the rightmost panel). Red dashed line shows Open-RS RS2 reported value~\cite{openRS_2025}; the gap from our base reflects eval-pipeline differences (see §ef{sec:limitations}).} \label{fig:arm_comparison} \end{figure*}  egin{figure*}[t] \centering \includegraphics[width=0.95	extwidth]{figures/fig2_training_curves.pdf} \caption{Per-step training rewards over 50 GRPO steps for each arm. \emph{Left:} $R_1$ (correctness) oscillates around 0.20 across all arms with no clear uptrend in 50 steps. \emph{Middle:} $R_2$ (format regex match) stays at 0 for all arms because DeepSeek-R1-Distill chat template auto-prepends 	exttt{<think>} to the assistant turn (so the opening tag is in the prompt, not the completion); the strict regex requires both opening and closing tags in the completion. \emph{Right:} $R_5$ fires at $\approx 1.0$ throughout \armC{} training because the model rarely code-switches on English prompts. Despite this constant signal, $R_5$ produces measurable downstream effects (Fig.~ef{fig:effect_sizes}). \armB{} (	extsc{vi}) curves omitted from the present version due to a missing log sync; will be added in revision.} \label{fig:training_curves} \end{figure*}  egin{figure}[t] \centering \includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_effect_sizes.pdf} \caption{Effect of each post-training arm relative to the untrained base (positive = improvement, negative = degradation). \armA{}'s AMC23 gain comes bundled with a $-16.7$ pp loss on AIME-2024 pass@1; \armC{}'s addition of $R_5$ almost completely closes that gap ($-3.4$ pp) while keeping the AMC23 gain ($+2.5$ pp). \armB{} sits between the two arms.} \label{fig:effect_sizes} \end{figure} \subsection{Finding 1: English-only GRPO causes benchmark-specific overfit} \label{sec:finding1} \armA{} gains $+7.5$ pp on AMC23 but loses $-16.7$ pp on AIME-2024 pass@1 relative to base (Table~ef{tab:delta_vs_base}). This is consistent with the ``peak-then-degrade'' dynamics reported by Open-RS but observed here at smaller absolute scale (50 steps from a distilled base). The model concentrates probability mass on AMC23-style intermediate-difficulty patterns and loses the depth-of-reasoning required for AIME's hardest problems. AIME maj@8 (which averages over 8 stochastic samples) shows a smaller drop ($-3.3$ pp), suggesting capability is preserved in the sample distribution but greedy decoding at \armA{}'s post-trained policy lands on shallower trajectories. \subsection{Finding 2: Vietnamese-translated training acts as a regularizer} \label{sec:finding2} \armB{} (VI training) shows the \emph{shape} of \armA{}'s degradation but at smaller magnitude: $+2.5$ pp on AMC23, $-10.0$ pp on AIME pass@1, $0.0$ on AIME maj@8. The model never overfits as aggressively because the training distribution (Vietnamese MetaMathQA) is sufficiently far from the AMC23 evaluation distribution. AIME maj@8 is fully preserved relative to base. We interpret this as \emph{out-of-domain training acting as natural regularization}. \subsection{Finding 3: Lang-consistency reward acts as implicit regularizer} \label{sec:finding3} \armC{} adds $R_5$ to the same English training data as \armA{}, with no other change. The result reverses \armA{}'s catastrophic AIME degradation: $+13.3$ pp pass@1 \emph{over \armA{}}, only $-3.4$ pp from base; AIME maj@8 \emph{exceeds} base by $+3.4$ pp. \armC{} also achieves the highest mean accuracy across all four metrics (43.3\% vs base 42.4\%, vs \armA{}'s 39.1\%). Critically, $R_5$ fires uniformly at 1.0 on \armA{}'s English training prompts because the model rarely code-switches on English. The mean reward shifts from $\mathbb{E}[R_1+R_2]\approx 0.2$ in \armA{} to $\mathbb{E}[R_1+R_2+R_5]\approx 1.2$ in \armC{}. Because GRPO computes per-group advantage as $(r -  ar{r})/	ext{std}(r)$, the mean shift cancels; only the variance changes. However, total reward magnitude affects PPO clipping ratios, KL trade-offs, and gradient norms, all of which are \emph{magnitude-sensitive} in the actual implementation. Our hypothesis: the auxiliary reward acts as an implicit regularizer by changing optimization geometry, not by injecting content signal. \subsection{Bringing all three findings together} Both \armB{} (different training distribution) and \armC{} (auxiliary reward component) recover or exceed base performance on AIME-2024, while \armA{} (vanilla EN-only GRPO) underperforms base by $-3.3$ pp on the 4-metric mean. The two interventions act through different mechanisms --- distribution shift in \armB{}, optimization-geometry shift in \armC{} --- but converge to the same qualitative effect: \emph{prevention of benchmark-specific overfit at 50 steps}. % ===================================================================== \section{Discussion} \label{sec:discussion} \paragraph{Why does \armC{} succeed despite $R_5$ being trivially satisfied?} The standard view is that an auxiliary reward must inject useful content signal. Our observation contradicts this: $R_5$ provides essentially no signal (uniform 1.0) yet substantially improves the model. We attribute this to PPO clipping and KL geometry. Higher mean reward $ ar{r}$ scales the advantage normalization, which interacts with the trust-region width via the importance-sampling ratio $r_t = \pi_	heta/\pi_{	heta_{	ext{old}}}$. If $ ar{r}$ is small, advantages are near zero for in-distribution samples; gradient updates focus on the few standout correct generations and overfit to their patterns. With higher $ ar{r}$, the advantage-zero baseline is wider and updates are more uniformly spread. This is testable: ablation with a constant additive bias on $R_1+R_2$ should reproduce the regularization effect. We leave this for future work. \paragraph{Implications for low-resource settings.} Practitioners often assume that translating training data into a target language ($\armB$) is the main lever for cross-lingual transfer. Our results suggest a cheaper alternative: keep the original training data, add a language-consistency auxiliary reward. The R5 mechanism requires no translation infrastructure, no additional training data, and yields better mean accuracy than translation in our single experiment. % ===================================================================== \section{Limitations} \label{sec:limitations} 	extbf{Single seed.} All numbers reported are from 	exttt{seed=42}. The largest effect sizes (\armA{}'s $-16.7$ pp on AIME, \armC{}'s $+13.3$ pp recovery) substantially exceed plausible single-cell variance estimates ($\pm 5$ pp), but mean differences ($\sim 1$ pp) are not statistically distinguishable. We commit to a multi-seed extension prior to camera-ready. 	extbf{LoRA-only training.} Open-RS used full-parameter on 4$	imes$A40; we use LoRA $r{=}16$ on 1$	imes$A100 to fit at the same effective batch size of 96. This explains our 22.5 pp gap to Open-RS reported AMC23 (57.5\% vs 80.0\%). Our findings about \emph{relative} arm comparisons may interact with LoRA's limited update capacity in unknown ways. 	extbf{\armB{} dataset is not a faithful translation of \armA{}.} \armB{} uses 	exttt{5CD-AI/Vietnamese-MetaMathQA-40K-gg-translated}, a Google-translated MetaMathQA, not a translation of the same Open-RS subset used in \armA{}. Confounds between dataset content and language make our Finding 2 a language-\emph{plus}-distribution effect rather than a clean language-only effect. 	extbf{Eval pipeline gap.} Even with the Open-RS 	exttt{lighteval} template verbatim, our base evaluation is $-12.9$ pp on AMC23 and $-23.4$ pp on MATH-500 relative to Open-RS reported. The gap is unexplained; we report all numbers vs \emph{our} base rather than Open-RS-reported numbers. 	extbf{	exttt{maj@4} on AMC23 is zero across all conditions} due to a yet-undiagnosed bug in our majority-vote sampling. We report 	exttt{pass@1} on AMC23 and 	exttt{pass@1}/	exttt{maj@8} on AIME-2024. 	extbf{Single base model and scale.} Results are on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B only. Generalization to Qwen2.5-Instruct or 3B+ untested. 	extbf{No multilingual evaluation yet.} The paper's full cross-lingual claim requires MGSM and MSVAMP eval, currently in progress. The present manuscript focuses on the English benchmark behavior under the three training arms. % ===================================================================== \section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion} We compared three GRPO arms at sub-3B scale on a single-GPU LoRA budget: pure English (\armA{}), Vietnamese-translated (\armB{}), and English plus a fastText-based language-consistency reward (\armC{}). \armA{} exhibits benchmark-specific overfit ($+7.5$ AMC23 / $-16.7$ AIME-2024). \armB{} and \armC{} both prevent this overfit, with \armC{} achieving the highest mean accuracy. Notably, \armC{}'s $R_5$ provides no content signal on English data (it fires at 1.0 nearly everywhere) yet still produces substantial regularization, suggesting that the regularization mechanism is a reward-magnitude effect on PPO optimization geometry rather than a learned content effect. We release all code, configs, LoRA adapters, and per-step reward traces. % =====================================================================  ibliographystyle{acl_natbib}  ibliography{refs} \appendix \input{appendix.tex} \end{document}
